{
  "fixture_version": 1,
  "kind": "local_transport_wire",
  "status": "proposed",
  "description": "The wire format of the service's deployment-local bootstrap and recovery transport (service/src/local_transport, module docs \"Protocol\", \"Operations\" and \"Errors\"): the request members of each op, the reply shapes, the closed set of refusal codes, the framing and the size limits, with golden request and reply lines. Inferred: no contract text fixes this wire format (the service's module docs say so); the service's code is the source, and status stays \"proposed\" until a contract states it. Framing: one request per connection; the client writes one JSON object on one line, the newline, then closes its write half (shutdown(SHUT_WR)); no byte may follow the newline; the server answers one JSON object on one line with its newline and closes. Requests: op first, then the op's members in the order listed under ops, no whitespace, byte values in lowercase hexadecimal, strings escaped as serde_json escapes them. Replies: serde_json's compact output, each success with exactly its members in the order listed under replies, every refusal {\"error\":\"<code>\"} and nothing else. framing.max_request_bytes is the service's MAX_REQUEST_BYTES; framing.max_reply_bytes is the client's own cap, 4 MiB (inferred: the service sets none, and a restore's revoked_credential_ids lists every credential it revoked, so its reply has no bound of its own); a reply over it is an unknown outcome (reconcile first). default_limits are the service's default time limits (Settings::default(): deadline, transaction_timeout and idle_timeout, in whole seconds) and the client's default total wait, their sum: the longest an accepted connection lasts on a default server, from the accept (the server's settings are tunable and not discoverable by the client); operator/tests/local_exchange and tests/harness/local_transport_wire.rs read them. The golden lines of exchange are the bytes the real service sent, with the given ID seed (SequentialIds) and clock, for a fresh database: the preview and restore signature is over the issued challenge M by the bootstrap recovery key (a test key). To regenerate exchange and the golden lines it names (the requests and replies of bootstrap, issue_challenge, preview and restore, and the not_restorable and conflict replies; every ID in them is a SequentialIds ID of exchange.id_seed): add for the time a test to tests/harness (wired in tests/pg.rs, run once with the database environment tests/pg.rs documents, then removed) that serves the service's LocalTransport::new(pool, a clock fixed at exchange.now_micros, SequentialIds::with_seed(exchange.id_seed), None), default Settings, on a fresh database (skyoauth-scenarios' TestDb, migrated), sends the requests of exchange.steps in order, each on its own connection framed as above, and prints each reply line verbatim. The requests are built from test keys: the bootstrap recovery key is the Ed25519 public key of the seed of 32 bytes 0x51 (SEED in service/tests/local_transport.rs), the new recovery key that of 32 bytes 0x52 (NEW_SEED); credential_spki is the Ed25519 SubjectPublicKeyInfo prefix 302a300506032b6570032100 followed by 32 bytes 0x01 (bootstrap) or 0x06 (issue_challenge), each a valid Ed25519 point (the service refuses a key that is not one); trust_anchor_ref is deployment/operator-ca and instance_id the bootstrap line's; the preview and restore signature is the 0x51 key's Ed25519 signature over the bytes of the challenge the issue_challenge reply carries. Ed25519 signatures are deterministic, so the same inputs give the same lines; a change to the ID format, to the order in which the transport draws IDs, to M or to a reply shape changes them, and tests/harness/local_transport_wire.rs (the_golden_exchange_is_the_services) fails until they are regenerated. issue_challenge_escapes and unknown_replies are written by hand, not captured. unknown_replies are bytes the operator's client must classify as an unknown outcome (reconcile first), with the reason it gives. Checked by operator/tests/local_wire (skyoauth-operator, every member) and tests/harness/local_transport_wire.rs (skyoauth-scenarios, against the service: constants, codes, golden requests through the real transport with an unreachable store, and the exchange on a database).",
  "framing": {
    "line_end": "\n",
    "max_request_bytes": 65536,
    "max_reply_bytes": 4194304
  },
